1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,473
Imagine standing on the side of a smooth hill. In single-variable calculus,

2
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:13,946
a curve has only one slope at each point. On a two-variable surface, however,

3
00:00:13,946 --> 00:00:18,000
your steepness depends entirely on the direction you choose to walk.

4
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:31,770
Step one way and you climb rapidly; step along a contour and your height barely changes.

5
00:00:31,770 --> 00:00:34,814
How do we measure the rate of change in any direction,

6
00:00:34,814 --> 00:00:37,720
and find the single direction of steepest ascent?

7
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:51,936
We begin with partial derivatives. Holding y fixed,

8
00:00:51,936 --> 00:00:56,972
the partial derivative with respect to x measures the slope purely in the x-direction.

9
00:00:56,972 --> 00:00:57,938
Holding x fixed,

10
00:00:57,938 --> 00:01:02,560
the partial derivative with respect to y measures the slope in the y-direction.

11
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:16,574
Packing these two coordinate slopes together into a vector gives the gradient of f,

12
00:01:16,574 --> 00:01:18,060
written as del f of x, y.

13
00:01:24,800 --> 00:01:27,915
To measure slope in any arbitrary direction u,

14
00:01:27,915 --> 00:01:33,187
we require u to be a unit vector of length one, so we measure change per unit step.

15
00:01:33,187 --> 00:01:37,420
The directional derivative is then the dot product: del f dot u.

16
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:52,758
Why does the gradient always point in the direction of steepest increase?

17
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:54,888
Rewrite the dot product using theta,

18
00:01:54,888 --> 00:01:58,940
the angle between the gradient vector and our unit direction vector u.

19
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:08,193
Because u has length one,

20
00:02:08,193 --> 00:02:13,453
the directional derivative simplifies to the magnitude of the gradient times cosine of

21
00:02:13,453 --> 00:02:13,880
theta.

22
00:02:19,400 --> 00:02:21,827
As u rotates around the unit circle,

23
00:02:21,827 --> 00:02:28,055
cosine theta reaches its maximum value of one only when theta equals zero degrees—meaning u

24
00:02:28,055 --> 00:02:31,533
points in the exact same direction as the gradient.

25
00:02:31,533 --> 00:02:34,040
The maximum slope is the norm of del f.

26
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:45,589
When theta is ninety degrees, cosine theta is zero,

27
00:02:45,589 --> 00:02:50,247
so walking tangent to a level curve produces zero instantaneous height change.

28
00:02:50,247 --> 00:02:54,140
At one hundred eighty degrees, you descend as steeply as possible.

29
00:03:03,267 --> 00:03:08,452
Let us work step by step through a concrete example: the paraboloid f of x,

30
00:03:08,452 --> 00:03:13,127
y equals x squared plus y squared, evaluated at the point one, two.

31
00:03:20,487 --> 00:03:25,677
First, compute both partial derivatives: partial f partial x equals two x,

32
00:03:25,677 --> 00:03:29,466
and partial f partial y equals two y. Evaluating at one,

33
00:03:29,466 --> 00:03:32,267
two gives the gradient vector two, four.

34
00:03:38,667 --> 00:03:42,723
Next, choose the direction u equals three-fifths, four-fifths.

35
00:03:42,723 --> 00:03:47,737
Its length is the square root of nine twenty-fifths plus sixteen twenty-fifths,

36
00:03:47,737 --> 00:03:50,687
which equals one—confirming u is a unit vector.

37
00:03:55,867 --> 00:04:01,128
Taking the dot product gives two times three-fifths plus four times four-fifths,

38
00:04:01,128 --> 00:04:04,178
which equals six-fifths plus sixteen-fifths,

39
00:04:04,178 --> 00:04:08,067
or twenty-two fifths—a slope of four point four. Meanwhile,

40
00:04:08,067 --> 00:04:11,727
the maximum possible slope at this point is two root five,

41
00:04:11,727 --> 00:04:14,167
approximately four point four seven.

42
00:04:21,767 --> 00:04:27,227
Geometrically, the level curves of x squared plus y squared are concentric circles centered

43
00:04:27,227 --> 00:04:28,067
at the origin.

44
00:04:33,467 --> 00:04:36,421
At the point one, two, the gradient vector two,

45
00:04:36,421 --> 00:04:41,496
four points radially outward—perpendicular to the circle of radius root five.

46
00:04:41,496 --> 00:04:44,601
Walking tangent to the circle gives zero change,

47
00:04:44,601 --> 00:04:47,707
while walking radially outward climbs fastest.

48
00:04:53,667 --> 00:04:56,439
Because our chosen direction three-fifths,

49
00:04:56,439 --> 00:05:01,765
four-fifths is separated from the gradient by approximately ten point three degrees,

50
00:05:01,765 --> 00:05:07,017
its slope of four point four captures almost all of the maximum steepness of four point

51
00:05:07,017 --> 00:05:07,747
four seven.

52
00:05:15,867 --> 00:05:21,071
To recap: the gradient del f combines the partial derivatives and always points in the

53
00:05:21,071 --> 00:05:23,095
direction of steepest increase,

54
00:05:23,095 --> 00:05:25,047
perpendicular to level curves.

55
00:05:30,407 --> 00:05:34,570
For any unit direction u, the directional derivative is del f dot u,

56
00:05:34,570 --> 00:05:39,327
bounded between negative and positive norm of del f. Thank you for watching.
